## Why

Live read-only production inspection on 8 Oct 2026 found 46 `public` tables with:

- RLS enabled;
- zero RLS policies (therefore normal row access is already default-deny);
- historical table privileges still granted to both `anon` and `authenticated`.

Each browser role currently has 190 table-grant rows across those 46 tables. Thirteen tables include full SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE grants; the rest still retain REFERENCES/TRIGGER/TRUNCATE. Raw `pg_attribute.attacl` inspection found zero explicit column ACLs.

PostgreSQL RLS does not govern whole-table privileges such as TRUNCATE and REFERENCES. Leaving those grants serves no current product path because a table with RLS and no policy is already intentionally closed to browser row access.

## Change

Add `20261008110000_revoke_browser_privileges_from_closed_rls_tables.sql`.

The migration:

- operates only in schema `public`;
- requires RLS to be enabled;
- requires zero policies on the table;
- revokes **all table privileges** from `anon` and `authenticated`;
- does not touch `service_role`;
- does not add/drop/alter an RLS policy;
- refuses vacuous success;
- fails replay if any browser table privilege survives;
- fails if an explicit column ACL exists on a closed RLS table, forcing review instead of silently assuming a table-level revoke was complete.

A future migration that intentionally exposes one of these tables must explicitly add both a policy and the minimum SQL grants.

