## Engineering objective

Close one measured SONARA capability gap without widening background device authority.

`POST /api/motion/events` existed and persisted tenant-scoped motion rows, while the repository's own form-reachability evidence correctly said no browser client had ever called it. This PR connects that route to the existing signed-in `/settings/device-feedback` surface through an explicit, bounded, privacy-reduced user action.

## What changes

- add `lib/sonara-motion-sample.cjs` as the server-owned normalization boundary;
- add `public/sonara-motion-capture.js` as the explicit foreground-only client;
- make motion and orientation permission requests independent in the shared device helper;
- add a page-scoped Permissions-Policy for accelerometer/gyroscope on `/settings/device-feedback` only;
- remove the page's inline event handler and keep feedback controls compatible with the existing `script-src 'self'` CSP;
- normalize the API against the database event vocabulary;
- round stored sensor values to one decimal place and reject non-finite/out-of-domain samples;
- derive organization/user only from authenticated server context;
- cap client sampling at 5 seconds, 10 Hz and 50 accepted samples;
- upload one aggregate summary, not individual sensor events;
- stop on cancel, page hide or page leave and abort an in-flight save;
- update reachability evidence and add permanent tests;
- add `docs/device/CONSENTED_MOTION_CAPTURE_2026-10-09.md`.

## Research basis

The implementation follows the W3C Device Orientation and Motion permission/privacy model and uses the Page Visibility lifecycle boundary to stop foreground work when the page becomes hidden.

## Explicit non-goals

No background tracking, gesture recognition, health/fall detection, route tracking, animation mocap, worker queue, provider integration, new database table, mobile-native sensor service, or production activation.

## Release boundary

Production is intentionally OFFLINE. This PR must not trigger a production deployment. Keep it draft until exact-head CI proves the source, generated capability inventory, browser/security checks and database contracts. If generated capability artifacts are stale, regenerate them rather than weakening the checks.

